<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zaranj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zarang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zaranj is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, capital of Nimruz province, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. As the chief town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> in the early Islamic centuries, it was the base from which the Saffarid ruler Ya'qub ibn al-Layth rose to power in 861, and later the seat of the Nasrid and Mihrabanid maliks. The medieval city was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1383.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaranj.jpg" alt="Zaranj" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sgt. Mallory S. VanderSchans, U.S. Marine Corps · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afghanistan-Iran_border_in_Zaranj,_Afghanistan,_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zaranj lies in the western part of Nimruz Province in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, near the Iranian border, at about 476 metres above sea level. Its water comes from the Khash and Helmand rivers. The climate is hot desert, with very hot summers when temperatures may approach 50 °C, cool winters and very little rain. The city stands on an important trade route linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and the Pul-i-Abresham border crossing lies in its west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Zaranj bears the name of an ancient city, attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> as Zranka and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> as Zrang, which in Greek became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drangiana">Drangiana</a>. The word derives from the Old Persian zaranka, &quot;waterland&quot;. The Achaemenid Zranka, capital of Drangiana, was almost certainly at Dahan-e Gholaman, southeast of Zabol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. After that city was abandoned its name passed to later administrative centres of the region, which came to be known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sakastan</a>, then Sijistan and finally Sistan. Medieval Zaranj stood at Nad-i Ali, 4.4 km north of the modern city. According to Arab geographers, it was founded by people who left Ram Shahristan, the earlier capital of Sistan, after its dam on the Helmand canal broke.</p><p>Zaranj surrendered to the governor of Khurasan in 652 and became a base for further caliphal expansion. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christian</a> community is recorded there in the sixth century, and a Jacobite diocese by the end of the eighth. In the 9th century it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar</a>, a local coppersmith turned warlord. Defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> in 900, the Saffarids were conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> in 1003. Zaranj later served as the capital of the Nasrid (1029-1225) and Mihrabanid (1236-1537) maliks of Nimruz. In 1738 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> removed the Hotak dynasty, who had captured it in the early 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Zaranj is the capital of Nimruz Province, which was separated from Farah-Chakansur Province in 1968 to form Nimruz and Farah. Nimruz University is the largest of its universities. Ethnic groups were reported in 2007 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baloch">Baloch</a> 44%, Pashtun 34% and Tajik 22%.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zaranj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The family claimed descent from the Saffarids and ruled from <b>Zaranj</b> and later other centres of the Helmand delta.</blockquote><a href="/state/mihrabanid-dynasty">Mihrabanid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1029 Abu'l-Fadl Nasr ibn Ahmad established himself at <b>Zaranj</b>, founding a line of maliks often called the Nasrids or Maliks of Nimruz.</blockquote><a href="/state/nasrid-maliks-of-sistan">Nasrid Maliks of Sistan</a></li><li><blockquote>Ya'qub ibn al-Layth, a coppersmith (saffar) turned leader of the ayyar volunteer bands of Sistan, took control of <b>Zaranj</b> in 861 while fighting Kharijites.</blockquote><a href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
